    ootCaptureCaption: "Bmori1_room_0, camera locked at ROOM_ANGLE 0, melonDS 1.1 in DS mode at 67 MHz. The bottom screen shows telemetry. Emulator capture, not a physical DSi.",
    ootConceptTag: "CONCEPT ART · NOT EVIDENCE",
    ootConceptOneAlt: "Digital artwork of a young man in a green tunic and hat, seen from behind with a shield on his back, standing between vine-covered stone ruins; ahead, a sword set in a pedestal bearing the Triforce symbol, and in the background a green valley, a castle and mountains under a low sun",
    ootConceptOneCaption: "AI-generated concept art — not a capture of the port. It shows the atmosphere driving the project; the real state remains the captures and measurements on this page.",
    ootConceptTwoAlt: "Digital artwork of glowing golden triangles, some wireframe and some reflecting foliage, breaking away from a wireframe structure on the left and drawn into an open handheld console on the right, while a blue fairy flies through the air; green ruins with waterfalls in the background",
    ootConceptTwoCaption: "AI-generated concept art — not a capture of the port. It is a visual metaphor for translating triangles between two pipelines, not a technical diagram or renderer output; the actual mechanism is described in the lists above and in the engineering studies.",
    ootConceptThreeAlt: "Digital artwork of a two-screen handheld console resting on a mossy stone block: the top screen shows a golden triangle over green clouds and the bottom screen shows glowing symbols arranged in a circle, with a blue ocarina, a shield and a stylus pen beside it, in front of ruins and a distant castle",
    ootConceptThreeCaption: "AI-generated concept art — not a capture of the port. The drawn screens are not a shipped interface: the project's two-screen layout reserves 3D for the top screen and telemetry for the bottom one.",
    ootIntroEyebrow: "01 / PRESENTATION",
    ootIntroTitle: "An investigation, not a finished port<span class=\"heading-period\">.</span>",
    ootIntroLead: "The project translates the N64 graphics pipeline to the DS and gradually integrates the game's systems. What exists today is a measured technical base — not the whole game.",
    ootIntroP1: "This is an experimental project to natively adapt <em>The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time</em> to the Nintendo DSi. The starting point is the game's C decompilation; the port interprets F3DEX2 display lists and translates them to the DS geometry engine.",
    ootIntroP2: "It is not an emulator, not a distributable ROM, and not a complete game. What exists today is a technical base: one real room rendered in the emulator, with the graphics engine and game integration growing on separate fronts.",
    ootIntroP3: "Everything on this page was recorded under <strong>OTD-NNN</strong> identifiers. Emulator results are presented as emulator results; no delivery has been validated on physical DSi hardware.",
    ootChallengeEyebrow: "02 / THE CHALLENGE",
    ootChallengeTitle: "Translating the N64 to the DS without pretending they are the same machine<span class=\"heading-period\">.</span>",
    ootChallengeLead: "The game expects an RSP and an RDP. The DS offers a geometry engine, two screens, and an ARM9 without an FPU. The port has to rebuild the path between those two worlds.",
    ootChallengeOneTitle: "Translate, don't emulate",
    ootChallengeOneText: "Each F3DEX2 display list is decoded and re-emitted in the DS format: matrices, vertices, textures, combiner, viewport, and clipping. No whole N64 running in software.",
    ootChallengeTwoTitle: "Do math without an FPU",
    ootChallengeTwoText: "The DSi's ARM9 has no floating-point unit. Camera, projection, matrices, and texture selection run in fixed point, with 64-bit multiplications where precision demands it.",
    ootChallengeThreeTitle: "Fit into two screens and little memory",
    ootChallengeThreeText: "3D takes the top screen; the 2D interface and telemetry stay on the bottom. Logic runs at 20 Hz, drawing has its own budget, and assets are read by streaming.",
    ootChallengeCaveatStrong: "One rendered room is not the game.",
    ootChallengeCaveatText: "It proves that the path from a real display list to the panel exists; it does not prove actors, collision, game camera, audio, progression, or physical hardware.",
    ootStateEyebrow: "03 / CURRENT STATE",
    ootStateTitle: "What stands, where it was tested, and what is missing<span class=\"heading-period\">.</span>",
    ootStateLead: "Each delivery has one state. “Tested on emulator” does not mean physical hardware; “implemented” does not mean tested.",
    ootStateDoneTitle: "Implemented and tested on emulator",
    ootStateDoneOne: "DSi environment probe: clock, heap, RAM, and both screens.",
    ootStateDoneTwo: "Loop with hardware timing, 20 Hz logic, memory arena, and telemetry.",
    ootStateDoneThree: "DS input into the BTN_* contract and streamed asset reads from the SD card.",
    ootStateDoneFour: "F3DEX2 decoder, matrices, projection, per-type stack, and viewport.",
    ootStateDoneFive: "CI4, CI8, and RGBA16 textures, LRU cache, cycle 0 combiner, alpha, and blending.",
    ootStateDoneSix: "Near-plane clipping and real game rooms: market_day_room_0 and Bmori1_room_0.",
    ootStateDoneSeven: "Per-triangle mip chain, vertex color in the combiner, and two-texture lerp in a second pass.",
    ootStatePartialTitle: "Partial or open",
    ootStatePartialOne: "Combiner cycle 1, ENV/PRIMITIVE sources, G_TT_IA16, rectangular textures, wrap/mask/shift, and clipping at x = ±w and y = ±w.",
    ootStatePartialTwo: "20,808 triangles (14.9%) require RSP lighting and remain outside the path.",
